  m_Name: Truss
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  lessonId: LESSON004
  lessonTitle: What is Truss
  lessonImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: c7ad14becd77b714a820300c6ed3a180, type: 3}
  popupDescription: "A <b><#E09500>Truss Bridge</color></b> uses connected structural
    members arranged in <b><#E09500>triangular patterns</color></b> to carry loads
    toward its supports. These arrangements help the bridge maintain its shape
    when forces act on it.\n\nThis lesson has been added to your
    <b><#E09500>Almanac</color></b>."
  almanacImage: {fileID: 0}
  lessonDescription: "A <b><#E09500>Truss Bridge</color></b> is a framework of connected
    structural members arranged in <b><#E09500>triangular patterns</color></b>.
    Loads entering through the roadway travel through these members toward the
    bridge supports.\n\nTriangular arrangements help the framework maintain its
    shape when forces act on it. They still require <b><#E09500>proper connections,
    suitable supports, and members with enough strength</color></b>; adding
    triangles does not make every bridge safe.\n\nIn an ideal pin-jointed truss,
    members mainly carry <b><#E09500>axial forces</color></b>: tension pulls and
    compression pushes along a member. Real members and connections can also
    experience bending. Later lessons explore these forces in more detail."
